  "id": "shipping_forecast_cadence",
  "name": "Shipping forecast cadence",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "The Met Office marine weather bulletin broadcast on BBC radio since the mid-1920s, read in a measured, deliberately calm cadence over long wave for mariners around the British Isles. The forecast names its 31 sea areas in a fixed roughly clockwise order — Viking, North Utsire, South Utsire, Forties, Cromarty, Forth, Tyne, Dogger, Fisher, German Bight and onward — within a strict word limit, so the litany's rhythm barely varies from bulletin to bulletin. The 0048 Radio 4 broadcast, preceded by the light-orchestral tune 'Sailing By', has become a secular bedtime liturgy for British listeners far beyond its maritime audience, its cadence quoted by poets and musicians alike. Delivery is neutral continuity-announcer speech: unhurried, evenly stressed, pitch-flat, with terminal falls closing each area's report.",
  "recipe": "classic British 1924-1926 shipping forecast cadence, spoken Word, broadcast branch numbers-station shortwave broadcast guided meditation. thick, low dynamic level, chest resonance low mid, unrestrained. am radio booth. large-diaphragm condenser, tube pre, German tube console. tape 15ips echo plate reverb.",
